course: dictionary
lesson: 8158
title: Learning a new word: resell
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To resell something means to sell it again after you have bought it. The word has two parts: re- means again, and sell means to give something to someone in return for money. So resell means sell again.
explain: First you buy something, then you sell it on. «Clothes given to clothes banks are sorted: good ones are resold.» The good clothes were bought once, then they are sold again to new people.
explain: Often the person reselling hopes to get more money than they paid. «A tout resells tickets for big money» shows this: the tout buys tickets, then sells them for more. In maths, the difference between the two prices is the profit.
fact: Resell means to sell something again after buying it.
fact: The prefix re- means again, so resell means sell again.
fact: «Resold» is the past tense: «Yesterday she resold her old bike.»
fact: In maths money problems, buying and reselling can give a profit (you gain money) or a loss (you lose money).
fact: «Resell for a profit» means you sell something for more money than you paid for it.
exercise: «Good clothes are resold, and many are shipped abroad.» What does resold mean? | Given away to a friend | Painted a new colour | Thrown away | *Sold again
exercise: Sam buys a ticket for 10 pounds and sells it for 25 pounds. What is he doing? | Giving the ticket away for free | Making a copy of the ticket | *Reselling the ticket for a profit | Buying a second ticket for himself
exercise: Which word is the past tense of resell? | *resold | resells | reselling | resell
test: «A tout resells tickets for big money.» What does resells mean here? | Keeps them for ever | *Sells again after buying them | Counts them carefully | Gives them to the team
test: Which word means to sell again after buying? | *Resell | Reheat | Rewrite | Refill
exam: Lia buys a painting for 40 pounds and resells it for 60 pounds. What does she gain? | A profit of 60 pounds | *A profit of 20 pounds | A loss of 60 pounds | Nothing at all
